(window.SERVICE_BANKS = window.SERVICE_BANKS || {})["identity"] = {"name":"Entra ID, Managed Identity & Key Vault","icon":"🔐","blurb":"Identity and secrets from A to Z: Microsoft Entra ID, app registrations and tokens, managed identities and workload identity federation, Azure RBAC, Entra authentication for Azure SQL, and Azure Key Vault with TDE and Always Encrypted keys.","order":4,"sourceNote":"Written from the Microsoft Learn documentation; every explanation links the page it was checked against.","sections":[{"id":"1","name":"Microsoft Entra ID"},{"id":"2","name":"Managed identities and workload identity"},{"id":"3","name":"Azure RBAC"},{"id":"4","name":"Microsoft Entra authentication for Azure SQL"},{"id":"5","name":"Azure Key Vault"},{"id":"6","name":"Database keys in Key Vault"}],"questions":[{"id":"identity-1.1-1","domain":2,"section":"1","source":"identity","sourceLabel":"Identity 1.1 · App registrations and service principals","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about application objects and service principals.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"The **application object** (App registrations) is the global definition with a unique app (client) ID; it has a one-to-many relationship with **service principals** (Enterprise applications), one per tenant. Registering in the admin center creates both automatically; with Microsoft Graph, creating the service principal is a separate step.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Application object = template:** registering `orders-api` in the Contoso tenant creates one application object with app (client) ID `1111...`, its redirect URIs, its app roles and its secrets or certificates.\n- **Service principal = local instance:** in each tenant, the service principal is what you assign roles to, what users consent to, and what appears in sign-in logs. You find it under **Enterprise applications**.\n- **One per consenting tenant:** when an admin at Fabrikam consents to a multitenant app, a service principal for app ID `1111...` appears in Fabrikam's tenant. The application object stays in Contoso.\n- **Never a service principal** is false for the admin center, which creates both. The CLI and Graph separate them:\n\n  ```bash\n  az ad app create --display-name orders-api    # application object only\n  az ad sp create --id <appId>                   # now the service principal\n  ```\n\n**Source:** [app objects and service principals](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity-platform/app-objects-and-service-principals)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["An application object lives only in the app's home tenant and acts as a template.","A service principal is the app's local representation in each tenant where it's used.","A multitenant app has one service principal in every tenant that consents to it.","Registering an app in the Microsoft Entra admin center creates the application object but never a service principal."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","Yes","No"]}},{"id":"identity-1.1-2","domain":2,"section":"1","source":"identity","sourceLabel":"Identity 1.1 · App registrations and service principals","scenario":"","stem":"Match each type of service principal to its description.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Users are represented by user principals and apps by service principals; both are security principals that can be granted access. Managed identities are a special type of service principal.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Application:** the service principal created for `orders-api` when it's registered or consented to. It has the same app ID as the registration.\n- **Managed identity:** when you turn on a web app's system-assigned identity, a service principal named after the web app appears under Enterprise applications (filter by *Managed Identities*), with no application object and no secret anyone can read.\n- **Legacy:** some old apps have only a service principal, with no app registration behind it, because they were created before app registrations existed. You can still manage their credentials and sign-in settings.\n- **A human user account** is a **user principal**, not a service principal. Both are security principals you can grant roles to, but users sign in interactively and service principals authenticate as apps.\n\n**Source:** [app objects and service principals](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity-platform/app-objects-and-service-principals)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["Application","Managed identity","Legacy"],"values":["A human user account","An app created before app registrations existed","Represents an Azure resource's identity with no credentials to manage","The local instance of a registered app in a tenant"],"answer":["The local instance of a registered app in a tenant","Represents an Azure resource's identity with no credentials to manage","An app created before app registrations existed"]}},{"id":"identity-1.2-1","domain":2,"section":"1","source":"identity","sourceLabel":"Identity 1.2 · Tokens","scenario":"","stem":"Match each token to its purpose.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Access tokens are short-lived (by default a random **60–90 minutes**, 75 on average) and are validated by the API they're for (check the `aud` audience). Use the Microsoft Authentication Library (MSAL) or Azure Identity to acquire, cache and refresh them.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Access token:** `GET https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/me` with `Authorization: Bearer eyJ0...`. Its `aud` claim names the API it's for, such as `https://database.windows.net/` for Azure SQL, and `scp` or `roles` lists what it allows. The API rejects a token meant for another audience.\n- **ID token:** after an OpenID Connect sign-in, the web app reads claims such as `name`, `preferred_username` and `oid` from it to show *\"Hello, Alice\"*. It isn't sent to APIs.\n- **Refresh token:** when the access token expires after about an hour, MSAL silently redeems the refresh token for a new one. Refresh tokens last much longer (90 days by default, 24 hours for single-page apps).\n- **Encrypts data at rest** isn't a token at all. That's the job of encryption keys, such as TDE's database encryption key or a customer-managed key in Key Vault.\n\n**Source:** [security tokens](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity-platform/security-tokens) · [access tokens](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity-platform/access-tokens)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["Access token","ID token","Refresh token"],"values":["Encrypts data at rest","Exchanged for new access tokens without signing in again","Sent as a bearer token to call a protected API","Tells the client app who signed in (OpenID Connect)"],"answer":["Sent as a bearer token to call a protected API","Tells the client app who signed in (OpenID Connect)","Exchanged for new access tokens without signing in again"]}},{"id":"identity-1.3-1","domain":2,"section":"1","source":"identity","sourceLabel":"Identity 1.3 · Conditional Access and Privileged Identity Management","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Conditional Access evaluates signals (user, location, device, app, risk) **after** first-factor authentication and grants, requires controls (such as MFA) or blocks. **PIM** makes privileged roles *eligible* rather than permanently active, with activation, approval and expiry.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **If-then policies:** for example, *if* a user in `Finance` signs in to the Azure portal from outside the trusted office locations, *then* require MFA and a compliant device.\n- **Before first-factor authentication** is false. Conditional Access needs to know *who* is signing in to evaluate the policy, so it runs after the password (or other first factor) is checked. Then it grants access, requires extra controls, or blocks.\n- **Block access:** for example, a policy that blocks every sign-in from countries where the company has no staff, or one that blocks legacy authentication protocols that can't do MFA.\n- **PIM:** Alice is *eligible* for SQL Server Contributor on the production resource group. To use it, she activates it for two hours with a justification (and approval, if the policy requires it). The role expires on its own afterwards.\n\n**Source:** [overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/conditional-access/overview) · [pim configure](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/id-governance/privileged-identity-management/pim-configure)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["Conditional Access policies are if-then statements, such as requiring MFA when a user signs in from a risky location.","Conditional Access is enforced before first-factor authentication.","Block access is the most restrictive Conditional Access decision.","Privileged Identity Management (PIM) provides just-in-time, time-bound activation of eligible roles."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","No","Yes","Yes"]}},{"id":"identity-2.1-1","domain":2,"section":"2","source":"identity","sourceLabel":"Identity 2.1 · System-assigned and user-assigned identities","scenario":"","stem":"Match each scenario to the managed identity type the docs recommend.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"A **system-assigned** identity is tied to one resource's lifecycle, and its name matches the resource. A **user-assigned** identity is a standalone resource you can attach to many resources and pre-authorize; it's recommended for Microsoft services and avoids hitting creation limits with ephemeral compute. Managed identities have no extra cost and no credentials you can see.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **System-assigned:** `az vm identity assign -g rg -n vm-batch` creates an identity named `vm-batch`. Deleting the VM deletes the identity, so nothing is left behind.\n- **User-assigned:** `az identity create -g rg -n id-web`, grant it a role once, then attach it to all twenty apps with `az webapp identity assign --identities <id-web resource ID>`. You can grant its roles before any app exists, and the identity survives when an app is deleted.\n- **Service principal with a secret** works, but someone has to store and rotate the secret, and it can leak. Managed identities have no credential you can see.\n- **Shared key** (such as a storage account key) isn't an identity at all. Anyone who has it has full access, and you can't tell who used it.\n\n**Source:** [overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/managed-identities-azure-resources/overview)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["One VM that needs access, with the identity deleted along with the VM","Twenty web apps that all need the same access","Grant a role before the resource that uses it is created","Rapidly creating and deleting many ephemeral resources"],"values":["Service principal with a secret","Shared key","System-assigned","User-assigned"],"answer":["System-assigned","User-assigned","User-assigned","User-assigned"]}},{"id":"identity-2.2-1","domain":2,"section":"2","source":"identity","sourceLabel":"Identity 2.2 · How a managed identity gets a token","scenario":"","stem":"Code on an Azure VM requests a token for its managed identity without any SDK. Which endpoint does it call?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"The **Azure Instance Metadata Service (IMDS)** endpoint is reachable only from inside the VM. Azure Resource Manager provisions the identity's certificate there, and IMDS exchanges it with Microsoft Entra ID for an access token for the requested `resource` (for example `https://storage.azure.com/`). Azure rotates the credentials for you.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **IMDS:**\n\n  ```bash\n  curl -H \"Metadata: true\" \\\n    \"http://169.254.169.254/metadata/identity/oauth2/token?api-version=2018-02-01&resource=https://database.windows.net/\"\n  ```\n\n  This returns JSON with `access_token` and `expires_on`. The address isn't routable from outside the VM, and requests without the `Metadata` header are rejected, which blocks server-side request forgery from tricking the app into calling it.\n- **`login.microsoftonline.com` with a client secret** is the client credentials flow for an app registration. A managed identity has no secret to send.\n- **`management.azure.com/identity/token`** doesn't exist. `management.azure.com` is Azure Resource Manager, which is something you'd call *with* a token, using `resource=https://management.azure.com/`.\n- **`localhost:8080`:** nothing listens there on a VM. App Service and Functions do use a local endpoint instead of IMDS, but its address comes from the `IDENTITY_ENDPOINT` environment variable, with an `X-IDENTITY-HEADER` secret, not from a fixed port.\n\n**Source:** [how managed identities work vm](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/managed-identities-azure-resources/how-managed-identities-work-vm)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"`http://169.254.169.254/metadata/identity/oauth2/token` with a `resource` parameter and the `Metadata: true` header"},{"key":"B","text":"`https://login.microsoftonline.com/<tenant>/oauth2/v2.0/token` with the client secret"},{"key":"C","text":"`https://management.azure.com/identity/token`"},{"key":"D","text":"`http://localhost:8080/token`"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"identity-2.3-1","domain":2,"section":"2","source":"identity","sourceLabel":"Identity 2.3 · Managed identity best practices","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each managed identity best practice statement.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Delete leftover role assignments yourself (they count toward subscription limits). Because groups and roles are claims in the access token, authorization changes don't take effect until the token is refreshed; app roles are an alternative to groups. Consider who can attach identities, because they inherit its access.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Role assignments removed automatically** is false. Delete a VM with a system-assigned identity, and its role assignments stay, shown in **Access control (IAM)** as *Identity not found*. Remove them with `az role assignment delete --assignee <principal-id>`.\n- **Cached tokens:** managed identity tokens are cached for up to 24 hours. Adding the identity to the `SQL-Readers` group today might not let it in until its token is refreshed. App roles or direct role assignments avoid relying on group claims.\n- **Least privilege:** a web app that only reads one container gets **Storage Blob Data Reader** on that container, not **Contributor** on the subscription.\n- **Assigning an identity = using it:** someone with **Managed Identity Operator** on `id-prod` and **Contributor** on a VM can attach `id-prod` to the VM and run code there with all of `id-prod`'s access. Treat the right to assign identities as a privilege.\n\n**Source:** [managed identity best practice recommendations](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/managed-identities-azure-resources/managed-identity-best-practice-recommendations)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["Role assignments are removed automatically when a managed identity is deleted.","Group membership changes for a managed identity can take effect only after its cached token is refreshed.","You should grant managed identities the least privilege they need.","A user who can assign an identity to a resource can effectively use that identity's permissions."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["No","Yes","Yes","Yes"]}},{"id":"identity-2.4-1","domain":2,"section":"2","source":"identity","sourceLabel":"Identity 2.4 · Workload identity federation","scenario":"","stem":"A GitHub Actions workflow and a Kubernetes cluster outside Azure need to access Azure resources without storing secrets. What do you configure?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"**Workload identity federation** lets an app registration or **user-assigned** managed identity trust tokens from GitHub, Kubernetes (AKS, EKS, GKE), Google Cloud, AWS and other OpenID Connect providers. The workload exchanges its own token for a Microsoft Entra token, so no secret is stored.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Workload identity federation:** a federated credential with issuer `https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com` and subject `repo:contoso/orders:ref:refs/heads/main`. The workflow asks GitHub for an OIDC token (`permissions: id-token: write`), and `azure/login@v2` exchanges it for an Entra token for the given `client-id`. Only that repository and branch can get in, and no secret is stored. For Kubernetes, the issuer is the cluster's OIDC issuer URL and the subject is a service account, such as `system:serviceaccount:orders:api`.\n- **A client secret in each platform** works, but it's a long-lived credential stored outside Azure that can leak and expires, so someone has to rotate it.\n- **A system-assigned managed identity on each external runner:** managed identities exist only for Azure resources (and Azure Arc-enabled servers). A GitHub-hosted runner or an EKS cluster can't have one.\n- **Shared access signatures** only work for Storage, are themselves secrets, and grant whatever the token allows to anyone who holds it.\n\n**Source:** [workload identity federation](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/workload-id/workload-identity-federation)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Workload identity federation: a federated credential on an app registration or user-assigned managed identity that trusts the external identity provider's tokens"},{"key":"B","text":"A client secret stored in each platform"},{"key":"C","text":"A system-assigned managed identity on each external runner"},{"key":"D","text":"Shared access signatures"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"identity-3.1-1","domain":2,"section":"3","source":"identity","sourceLabel":"Identity 3.1 · Role assignments and scopes","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about Azure RBAC.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Azure RBAC is **additive**: effective permissions are the sum of all role assignments. Deny assignments are checked first and block access. Roles list `Actions`/`NotActions` (control plane) and `DataActions`/`NotDataActions` (data plane, such as reading blobs or secrets).\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Principal + role + scope:** for example, *group `DBAs`* + *SQL Server Contributor* + *`/subscriptions/<id>/resourceGroups/rg-sql`*.\n- **Inheritance:** a Reader assignment on the subscription lets Alice read every resource group and resource in it, including ones created later.\n- **The most restrictive wins** is false. RBAC is additive: if Alice is Reader on the subscription and Contributor on `rg-sql`, she can change anything in `rg-sql` and only read everything else.\n- **Deny assignments** win over grants. You can't create them yourself. Azure creates them, for example for managed applications or deployment stacks with deny settings, so even an Owner can't delete the protected resources.\n\n**Source:** [overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/role-based-access-control/overview) · [scope overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/role-based-access-control/scope-overview)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["A role assignment is made of a security principal, a role definition and a scope.","Scopes are management group, subscription, resource group and resource, and assignments are inherited downward.","When a user has several role assignments, the most restrictive one wins.","A deny assignment blocks access even if a role assignment grants it."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","No","Yes"]}},{"id":"identity-3.2-1","domain":2,"section":"3","source":"identity","sourceLabel":"Identity 3.2 · Azure roles versus Microsoft Entra roles","scenario":"","stem":"Match each built-in role to what it allows.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"These are **Azure roles** for Azure resources. **Microsoft Entra roles** (such as Global Administrator or User Administrator) manage the directory itself: users, groups and apps. The two systems are separate. Role Based Access Control Administrator can assign roles with conditions.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Owner:** can create a SQL server *and* run `az role assignment create --assignee alice@contoso.com --role Reader --scope <rg>`.\n- **Contributor:** can create, change and delete the SQL server, but the same `az role assignment create` fails with *AuthorizationFailed*, because it lacks `Microsoft.Authorization/roleAssignments/write`.\n- **Reader:** can open the server's blades and see its settings, but can't change them. Reader doesn't give access to data either.\n- **User Access Administrator:** can grant Alice Contributor, but can't create a database itself.\n- **Manage Microsoft Entra users and groups** is a **Microsoft Entra role** (such as User Administrator), not an Azure role. No Azure role, not even Owner on every subscription, lets you create users or reset passwords in the directory.\n\n**Source:** [rbac and directory admin roles](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/role-based-access-control/rbac-and-directory-admin-roles)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["Owner","Contributor","Reader","User Access Administrator"],"values":["Manage Microsoft Entra users and groups","Manage all resources and assign roles","Manage all resources but not assign roles","Manage user access (assign roles), without managing resources","View resources only"],"answer":["Manage all resources and assign roles","Manage all resources but not assign roles","View resources only","Manage user access (assign roles), without managing resources"]}},{"id":"identity-4.1-1","domain":2,"section":"4","source":"identity","sourceLabel":"Identity 4.1 · The Entra admin, logins and users","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about Microsoft Entra authentication in Azure SQL.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"The Entra admin (a user, group, service principal or managed identity) is the first principal that can create other Entra logins and users. Entra identities can be **logins** (server principals), login-based users, or **contained database users** (`CREATE USER ... FROM EXTERNAL PROVIDER`). Only tokens from Microsoft Entra ID are accepted. Groups simplify permission management.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **An Entra admin is required:** until you run something like `az sql server ad-admin create -g rg -s contoso --display-name DBA-Group --object-id <group-id>`, nobody can sign in with Entra ID, because there's no one who can create the first Entra users.\n- **A group admin:** making `DBA-Group` the admin means adding or removing a DBA is a group membership change, with nothing to update in SQL.\n- **Server login:**\n\n  ```sql\n  -- in master\n  CREATE LOGIN [alice@contoso.com] FROM EXTERNAL PROVIDER;\n  -- in the user database\n  CREATE USER [alice@contoso.com] FROM LOGIN [alice@contoso.com];\n  ```\n\n  A contained user (`CREATE USER [alice@contoso.com] FROM EXTERNAL PROVIDER;` in the database) needs no login.\n- **Third-party identity providers** is false. A token issued by Okta or Google is rejected. Such users have to come through Entra ID, for example as B2B guests or through federation.\n\n**Source:** [authentication aad overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-sql/database/authentication-aad-overview)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["A Microsoft Entra administrator must be set on the server or managed instance to enable Entra authentication.","The Entra admin can be a group, so all its members get admin rights.","`CREATE LOGIN [user@contoso.com] FROM EXTERNAL PROVIDER` creates a Microsoft Entra server login.","Azure SQL accepts access tokens from third-party identity providers."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","Yes","No"]}},{"id":"identity-4.2-1","domain":2,"section":"4","source":"identity","sourceLabel":"Identity 4.2 · Microsoft Entra-only authentication","scenario":"","stem":"You enable Microsoft Entra-only authentication on an Azure SQL logical server. Answer Yes or No for each statement.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Entra-only authentication disables SQL authentication at the server or instance level. For **separation of duties**, SQL Server Contributor and SQL Managed Instance Contributor can't toggle it; Owners, Contributors and **SQL Security Manager** can.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **SQL logins stop working:** after `az sql server ad-only-auth enable -g rg -n contoso`, even the SQL admin created with the server fails to sign in with its password. The logins still exist and work again if the setting is turned off.\n- **Azure Policy:** the built-in policies such as *Azure SQL Database should have Microsoft Entra-only authentication enabled during creation* deny creating a server that allows SQL authentication.\n- **SQL Server Contributor can toggle it** is false. That role manages servers, but for separation of duties, it can't change this setting. Otherwise whoever manages the server could turn password logins back on.\n- **SQL Security Manager:** a security team with this role can turn Entra-only on or off without broader rights over the server.\n\n**Source:** [authentication azure ad only authentication](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-sql/database/authentication-azure-ad-only-authentication)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["SQL authentication logins, including the SQL server administrator, can no longer connect.","Azure Policy can require new servers to be created with Entra-only authentication.","The SQL Server Contributor role can enable or disable Entra-only authentication.","The SQL Security Manager role can manage the Entra-only setting."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","No","Yes"]}},{"id":"identity-4.3-1","domain":2,"section":"4","source":"identity","sourceLabel":"Identity 4.3 · Server identity and Microsoft Graph permissions","scenario":"","stem":"You assign a user-assigned managed identity as an Azure SQL server's identity so it can look up Entra users and groups. What permissions does that identity need?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"The server identity reads Microsoft Entra ID through **Microsoft Graph**. Grant it `User.Read.All`, `GroupMember.Read.All` and `Application.Read.All` (or the Directory Readers role), ideally before creating servers. A user-assigned identity lets you grant this once and reuse it across servers. SQL Managed Instance requires an instance identity for Entra authentication.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Graph permissions:** without them, `CREATE USER [alice@contoso.com] FROM EXTERNAL PROVIDER;` fails with a message that the server identity lacks permission to read the directory. After an admin grants `User.Read.All`, `GroupMember.Read.All` and `Application.Read.All` (for example with `New-MgServicePrincipalAppRoleAssignment`), the same statement works.\n- **Owner on the subscription** is an Azure RBAC role over resources. It gives no rights to read users or groups in Microsoft Entra ID.\n- **Key Vault Secrets User** lets an identity read secrets. The server needs Key Vault access only for customer-managed TDE, and then it needs key permissions (such as **Key Vault Crypto Service Encryption User**), not secrets.\n- **Nothing:** the identity has to read the directory to turn a name into an object ID, so it can't be nothing.\n\n**Source:** [authentication azure ad user assigned managed identity](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-sql/database/authentication-azure-ad-user-assigned-managed-identity)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Microsoft Graph application permissions such as `User.Read.All`, `GroupMember.Read.All` and `Application.Read.All`, or the Directory Readers role"},{"key":"B","text":"Owner on the subscription"},{"key":"C","text":"Key Vault Secrets User"},{"key":"D","text":"Nothing"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"identity-4.4-1","domain":2,"section":"4","source":"identity","sourceLabel":"Identity 4.4 · Connection string authentication modes","scenario":"","stem":"Match each scenario to the `Authentication=` value in a Microsoft.Data.SqlClient connection string.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Other modes: `Active Directory Integrated` (Windows integrated), `Device Code Flow`, `Workload Identity` (federated token file) and the deprecated `Active Directory Password` (don't use it for new apps). For a user-assigned identity, add `User Id=<client-id>`.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Managed Identity:** `Server=tcp:contoso.database.windows.net;Database=Sales;Authentication=Active Directory Managed Identity;` (add `User Id=<client-id>` for a user-assigned identity).\n- **Default:** tries several credentials in order: environment variables, workload identity, managed identity, then developer tools such as Visual Studio and the Azure CLI. The same string works on your laptop after `az login` and in Azure.\n- **Interactive:** opens a browser or sign-in window, so the user can do MFA. Fits SSMS-style desktop tools.\n- **Service Principal:** `Authentication=Active Directory Service Principal;User Id=<app-id>;Password=<secret>;`. A daemon that can't have a managed identity uses this.\n- **Active Directory Password** sends a username and password with no MFA, and is deprecated. Accounts with MFA or Conditional Access can't use it, so don't build new apps on it.\n\n**Source:** [azure active directory authentication](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/connect/ado-net/sql/azure-active-directory-authentication)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["An app on Azure App Service using its managed identity","Code that should work with `az login` locally and a managed identity in Azure","A desktop tool where users sign in with MFA","A daemon with a client ID and secret"],"values":["Active Directory Default","Active Directory Interactive","Active Directory Managed Identity","Active Directory Password","Active Directory Service Principal"],"answer":["Active Directory Managed Identity","Active Directory Default","Active Directory Interactive","Active Directory Service Principal"]}},{"id":"identity-5.1-1","domain":2,"section":"5","source":"identity","sourceLabel":"Identity 5.1 · Vaults, objects and tiers","scenario":"","stem":"Match each Key Vault object or tier to its description.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Vaults store secrets, keys and certificates. Standard vaults hold software-protected keys; **Premium** adds HSM-protected keys. Objects are versioned, and secrets have `nbf` (not before), `exp` (expiry) and `enabled` attributes. Managed HSM is a separate, single-tenant service.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Secret:** `az keyvault secret set --vault-name kv-orders --name SqlConn --value \"Server=...\"`. Apps read it back with `GET https://kv-orders.vault.azure.net/secrets/SqlConn`.\n- **Key:** `az keyvault key create --vault-name kv-orders --name tde --kty RSA --size 3072`. Azure SQL then calls `wrapKey` and `unwrapKey` on it, and the private key never leaves the vault.\n- **Certificate:** `az keyvault certificate create --vault-name kv-orders -n api-tls -p \"$(az keyvault certificate get-default-policy)\"` creates the certificate, its key and a policy that can renew it automatically before expiry.\n- **Premium tier:** `az keyvault create --sku premium` lets you create keys with `--kty RSA-HSM`, kept in HSMs.\n- **A Microsoft Entra group** isn't something a vault stores. Groups are directory objects, and you'd assign a group a Key Vault role.\n\n**Source:** [basic concepts](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/key-vault/general/basic-concepts) · [about keys](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/key-vault/keys/about-keys) · [about secrets](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/key-vault/secrets/about-secrets)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["Secret","Key","Certificate","Premium tier"],"values":["A Microsoft Entra group","A value such as a connection string or password, up to 25 KB","Adds HSM-protected keys","An RSA or EC key used for cryptographic operations without exporting the private part","An X.509 certificate with its key and policy"],"answer":["A value such as a connection string or password, up to 25 KB","An RSA or EC key used for cryptographic operations without exporting the private part","An X.509 certificate with its key and policy","Adds HSM-protected keys"]}},{"id":"identity-5.2-1","domain":2,"section":"5","source":"identity","sourceLabel":"Identity 5.2 · Control plane, data plane and Key Vault roles","scenario":"","stem":"Match each requirement to the least-privileged Key Vault built-in role (RBAC permission model).","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Key Vault has a **control plane** (manage the vault, always Azure RBAC) and a **data plane** (secrets, keys, certificates), authorized with Azure RBAC (recommended) or legacy **access policies**. Both authenticate with Microsoft Entra ID. Other roles: Secrets Officer, Certificates Officer, Crypto User, Purge Operator and Data Access Administrator.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Secrets User:** an app can read `SqlConn`'s value, but can't create, change or delete secrets, and can't touch keys or certificates.\n- **Crypto Service Encryption User:** a storage account's identity can read the key's metadata and wrap or unwrap with it, which is all customer-managed encryption needs.\n- **Crypto Officer:** the security team can create keys, set rotation policies, rotate and delete. It doesn't grant secret or certificate access.\n- **Reader:** the auditor can list secret names and see their attributes (enabled, expiry), but reading a value returns *Forbidden*.\n- **Key Vault Administrator** would work for every row, but it allows every data-plane operation on all secrets, keys and certificates. That's far more than any of these needs.\n\n**Source:** [rbac guide](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/key-vault/general/rbac-guide)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["An app reads a connection string secret","A storage account wraps and unwraps its customer-managed key","A security team creates and rotates keys","An auditor sees which secrets exist but not their values"],"values":["Key Vault Administrator","Key Vault Crypto Officer","Key Vault Crypto Service Encryption User","Key Vault Reader","Key Vault Secrets User"],"answer":["Key Vault Secrets User","Key Vault Crypto Service Encryption User","Key Vault Crypto Officer","Key Vault Reader"]}},{"id":"identity-5.3-1","domain":2,"section":"5","source":"identity","sourceLabel":"Identity 5.3 · Soft delete and purge protection","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about soft delete and purge protection.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Soft-deleted vaults and objects can be **recovered** during retention, and a soft-deleted vault's name can't be reused until then. **Purge protection** is optional and recommended for encryption keys; services such as TDE with customer-managed keys require it. Recovering a vault doesn't restore its RBAC assignments.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Soft delete is always on:** `az keyvault delete -n kv-orders` puts the vault in a deleted state, and `az keyvault recover -n kv-orders` brings it back with its secrets, keys and certificates.\n- **Retention 7–90 days, set at creation:** `az keyvault create ... --retention-days 30`. You can't change it later.\n- **Purge protection enabled by default** is false. You turn it on with `--enable-purge-protection true`, and once on it can't be turned off.\n- **No purge during retention:** with purge protection, `az keyvault purge -n kv-orders` fails until the retention period ends. Even an admin, or an attacker with admin rights, can't destroy the TDE key early. Without it, a purge permanently deletes the vault right away.\n\n**Source:** [soft delete overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/key-vault/general/soft-delete-overview)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["Soft delete is on by default for new vaults and can't be turned off.","The retention period is 7–90 days (default 90) and can only be set when the vault is created.","Purge protection is enabled by default.","With purge protection on, deleted vaults and objects can't be purged until the retention period ends."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","No","Yes"]}},{"id":"identity-5.4-1","domain":2,"section":"5","source":"identity","sourceLabel":"Identity 5.4 · Networking and throttling","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about Key Vault networking and limits.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Restrict access with the firewall, virtual network rules or **private endpoints** (and disable public access). Key Vault throttles clients that exceed limits with **429** responses, so cache secrets in memory for a while and retry with exponential backoff.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Firewall off by default:** a new vault accepts requests from any network, as long as the caller is authorized. Turn it on with `az keyvault update -n kv-orders --default-action Deny`.\n- **Trusted services:** `--bypass AzureServices` lets listed services, such as Azure SQL using a TDE key or Azure Backup, through the firewall. A web app's managed identity isn't a trusted service, so the app needs a network rule or a private endpoint.\n- **Private endpoints:** `kv-orders.vault.azure.net` resolves through the `privatelink.vaultcore.azure.net` zone to a VNet IP such as `10.0.2.4`. Combine it with `--public-network-access Disabled`.\n- **Read on every request** is false. An API reading `SqlConn` on every call can hit Key Vault's per-vault limits and get **429 Too Many Requests**. Read it once at startup, cache it for a while, and retry with exponential backoff.\n\n**Source:** [network security](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/key-vault/general/network-security) · [overview throttling](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/key-vault/general/overview-throttling)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["The Key Vault firewall is disabled by default for a new vault.","With the firewall on, you can allow trusted Microsoft services to bypass it, but not every Azure service is on that list.","Private endpoints let clients reach the vault over a private IP in a virtual network.","Applications should read secrets from Key Vault on every request instead of caching them."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","Yes","No"]}},{"id":"identity-5.5-1","domain":2,"section":"5","source":"identity","sourceLabel":"Identity 5.5 · Key Vault references and key rotation","scenario":"","stem":"An App Service app setting must read a secret from Key Vault without code changes. Complete the setting and the access it needs.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"**Key Vault references** (`@Microsoft.KeyVault(SecretUri=...)` or `VaultName=...;SecretName=...`) resolve secrets for app settings. They use the app's system-assigned identity by default, or a user-assigned one when the secret is needed at creation time. Grant **Key Vault Secrets User** (RBAC) or the Get secrets permission (access policy). Omitting the version picks up rotations.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`KeyUri`** isn't a parameter. References point at secrets (`SecretUri=...` or `VaultName=myvault;SecretName=SqlConn`), and a certificate's private key is reachable as a secret too.\n- **`VaultUri`** alone isn't enough: the reference needs a specific secret, not just the vault.\n- **Deployment slot:** slots are copies of the app with their own settings, not an identity. Each slot has its own managed identity, though, so a staging slot needs its own role assignment.\n- **Publish profile:** credentials for deploying code to the app. They don't authenticate to Key Vault.\n- **Key Vault Crypto User** covers keys, not secrets. **Key Vault Reader** reads metadata but not secret values. With either one, the setting shows a red error in the portal and the app sees the literal `@Microsoft.KeyVault(...)` string.\n\n**Source:** [app service key vault references](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/app-service/app-service-key-vault-references) · [how to configure key rotation](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/key-vault/keys/how-to-configure-key-rotation)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"template":"App setting value:\n  @Microsoft.KeyVault([[0]]=https://myvault.vault.azure.net/secrets/SqlConn/)\n\nThe app's [[1]] needs the [[2]] role on the vault.","options":[["KeyUri","SecretUri","VaultUri"],["deployment slot","managed identity","publish profile"],["Key Vault Crypto User","Key Vault Reader","Key Vault Secrets User"]],"answer":["SecretUri","managed identity","Key Vault Secrets User"]}},{"id":"identity-5.5-2","domain":2,"section":"5","source":"identity","sourceLabel":"Identity 5.5 · Key Vault references and key rotation","scenario":"","stem":"You want Key Vault to create a new version of a key automatically every 90 days and send an Event Grid event before expiry. What do you configure?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"A **key rotation policy** sets the rotation frequency, expiry and near-expiry Event Grid notifications. Managing it needs **Key Vault Crypto Officer** (RBAC) or the Rotate, Set Rotation Policy and Get Rotation Policy permissions. Services such as TDE with a customer-managed key can then pick up the new version automatically.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Rotation policy:**\n\n  ```bash\n  az keyvault key rotation-policy update --vault-name kv-orders -n tde --value '{\n    \"lifetimeActions\": [\n      {\"trigger\": {\"timeAfterCreate\": \"P90D\"}, \"action\": {\"type\": \"Rotate\"}},\n      {\"trigger\": {\"timeBeforeExpiry\": \"P30D\"}, \"action\": {\"type\": \"Notify\"}}],\n    \"attributes\": {\"expiryTime\": \"P1Y\"}}'\n  ```\n\n  Notify raises the `Microsoft.KeyVault.KeyNearExpiry` Event Grid event.\n- **A secret with `exp` set** only marks when a secret expires. It's the wrong object (a secret, not a key) and creates no new version.\n- **Soft delete** protects deleted objects. It doesn't rotate anything.\n- **A Key Vault reference** is how App Service reads a secret into an app setting, unrelated to key rotation.\n\n**Source:** [app service key vault references](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/app-service/app-service-key-vault-references) · [how to configure key rotation](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/key-vault/keys/how-to-configure-key-rotation)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"A key rotation policy on the key"},{"key":"B","text":"A secret with `exp` set"},{"key":"C","text":"Soft delete"},{"key":"D","text":"A Key Vault reference"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"identity-5.6-1","domain":2,"section":"5","source":"identity","sourceLabel":"Identity 5.6 · Managed HSM","scenario":"","stem":"Your compliance rules require a single-tenant, fully managed HSM pool validated to FIPS 140-3 Level 3, with its own local RBAC. Which service fits?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"**Managed HSM** is a fully managed, highly available, **single-tenant** HSM service. Key Vault Premium also uses HSMs, but in a multitenant service. Managed HSM has its own roles such as Managed HSM Crypto User and Crypto Service Encryption User.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Managed HSM:** `az keyvault create --hsm-name contoso-hsm --administrators <object-id> ...` creates an HSM pool dedicated to you. Before you can use it, you download its *security domain* (which you keep), and you grant access with its own local RBAC roles, such as Managed HSM Crypto User.\n- **Key Vault Standard** stores keys in software in a multitenant service. Premium adds HSM-backed keys, but the HSMs are still shared.\n- **Storage encryption scopes** let one storage account encrypt containers or blobs with different keys. They need a key store, such as Key Vault or Managed HSM, and aren't one.\n- **App Configuration** stores settings and feature flags (and Key Vault references to secrets). It isn't a key store or an HSM.\n\n**Source:** [overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/key-vault/managed-hsm/overview)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Azure Key Vault Managed HSM"},{"key":"B","text":"Key Vault Standard"},{"key":"C","text":"Azure Storage with encryption scopes"},{"key":"D","text":"Azure App Configuration"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"identity-6.1-1","domain":2,"section":"6","source":"identity","sourceLabel":"Identity 6.1 · TDE with customer-managed keys","scenario":"","stem":"You use a customer-managed key in Key Vault as the TDE protector for an Azure SQL logical server. Answer Yes or No for each statement.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"With customer-managed TDE, the protector in Key Vault or Managed HSM wraps the DEK of every encrypted database on the server (or instance; it can also be set per database). Revoking access makes databases inaccessible, which gives you control, but changes can take about **10 minutes** to apply. The vault needs soft delete and purge protection.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Protector wraps each DEK:** every database has its own DEK, encrypted (wrapped) with the protector key `https://kv-orders.vault.azure.net/keys/tde`. The DEK, not the protector, encrypts the data pages.\n- **get, wrapKey, unwrapKey:** in the RBAC model, give the server's identity **Key Vault Crypto Service Encryption User** on the key, then run `az sql server tde-key set -g rg -s contoso --server-key-type AzureKeyVault --kid <key-id>`.\n- **Revoking makes databases inaccessible:** remove that role or disable the key, and the databases on the server go into the *Inaccessible* state, so no one can read the data until access comes back. That's how you can cut off access to your data without Microsoft's help.\n- **Takes effect instantly** is false. Permission changes on the vault can take about **10 minutes** to reach the server, so don't expect revocation or restore to take effect the moment you click.\n\n**Source:** [transparent data encryption byok overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-sql/database/transparent-data-encryption-byok-overview)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["The TDE protector encrypts each database's database encryption key (DEK).","The server's identity needs get, wrapKey and unwrapKey permissions on the key.","If you revoke the server's access to the key, its databases become inaccessible.","Permission changes on the key vault take effect instantly."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","Yes","No"]}},{"id":"identity-6.2-1","domain":2,"section":"6","source":"identity","sourceLabel":"Identity 6.2 · Always Encrypted column master keys","scenario":"","stem":"Why is Azure Key Vault a good store for Always Encrypted column master keys?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"Column master keys protect the column encryption keys. **Local** stores (such as the Windows certificate store) must be replicated to every client machine; a **centralized** store like Key Vault serves them all. Client drivers with Always Encrypted support use the key (wrap/unwrap, sign/verify) through the Key Vault provider.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Centralized store:** the column master key's metadata in the database points at Key Vault:\n\n  ```sql\n  CREATE COLUMN MASTER KEY CMK_KV WITH (\n      KEY_STORE_PROVIDER_NAME = N'AZURE_KEY_VAULT',\n      KEY_PATH = N'https://kv-orders.vault.azure.net/keys/AE-CMK/<version>');\n  ```\n\n  Every app server with `Column Encryption Setting=Enabled` and the right Key Vault role can then decrypt, with no certificate to install on each machine.\n- **Stores the encrypted column data** is false. The ciphertext stays in the database's columns, and Key Vault holds only the key that protects the column encryption keys.\n- **Decrypts data inside SQL Server** is false. The client driver decrypts, so SQL Server never sees plaintext or the key. Only with secure enclaves can the engine compute on encrypted data, inside the enclave.\n- **Required for TDE** is false, and TDE is a separate feature anyway. TDE uses a service-managed key by default, and Key Vault only comes in if you choose a customer-managed TDE protector.\n\n**Source:** [create and store column master keys always encrypted](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/security/encryption/create-and-store-column-master-keys-always-encrypted)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"It's a centralized key store, so apps on many machines can use the key without copying it to each one"},{"key":"B","text":"It stores the encrypted column data"},{"key":"C","text":"It decrypts data inside SQL Server"},{"key":"D","text":"It's required for TDE"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""}]};
